            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did pātraṁ mean, and what did peṭṭi mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: കഴുത്ത്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കഴുത്ത് (kaḻuttŭ) — \"the neck\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Inherited, matching Tamil kaḻuttu, and carrying ഴ again — the third run of this book to lean on that sound, and by now your mouth should be finding it without being told."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is the neck and the throat together, outside and in. A garland goes round the കഴുത്ത് (kaḻuttŭ), and so does the thin gold chain a Kerala woman is given at her wedding."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The held tt and the closing ŭ are both here as well, which makes this word a short review of three things at once."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five more, and it holds the garland."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
