        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: മുതുക്",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "മുതുക് (mutukŭ) — \"the back\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Inherited, matching Tamil mutuku, and it names the back of a body — the ridged stretch between the shoulders and the waist."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malayalam separates that from the general idea of behindness, which it handles with a different word altogether. മുതുക് (mutukŭ) stays on the body, so it is the word for the back that aches after a day bent over a വയൽ."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It shows up in the ordinary phrase for carrying a child astride the hip or the back, which is how most Kerala infants travel."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two: the neck, and what runs down from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
